n\n\\[\nD_{s}=\\{(a,b)\\in S_{3}\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nD_{2}=\\{(1,1)\\},\\qquad D_{3}=\\{(1,2),(2,1)\\},\\qquad\nD_{4}=\\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry of a line with respect to the diagonals\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be any line in the plane.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) is **sunny** (its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), then the equations\n\\(\\ell\\) and \\(x+y=s\\) are independent for each \\(s\\); consequently \\(\\ell\\) meets each\ndiagonal \\(D_{s}\\) in **at most one** point.*\n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) is **non\u2011sunny**, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(y=c\\)),\nto the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(x=c\\)), or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)), then\n\n* a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) contains at most one point of each diagonal,\n  but it can contain **all** points of a diagonal whose points have that same\n  \\(y\\)-coordinate;\n* a vertical line \\(x=c\\) behaves analogously;\n* a line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) and therefore contains **all**\n  points of the diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) (if any).\n\nHence a non\u2011sunny line can contain either \\(0,1,2\\) or \\(3\\) points of \\(S_{3}\\);\nif it contains three points they must belong to the same diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists\n\nAssume that three distinct lines \\(\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) cover all points of\n\\(S_{3}\\) and that exactly two of them are sunny.  Relabel so that\n\\(\\ell_{1}\\) is the unique non\u2011sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny line cannot contain three points\n\nIf \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contained the three points of \\(D_{4}\\) (the only diagonal with\nthree points), then the remaining points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) would have to be\ncovered by the two sunny lines \\(\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\).\n\nSince a sunny line cannot contain two points of the same diagonal, each of\n\\(\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\) could contain at most one of the points of \\(D_{3}\\).\nConsequently one of the sunny lines would have to contain both \\((1,1)\\) and a\npoint of \\(D_{3}\\).  The only possibilities are the pairs\n\\((1,1)-(1,2)\\) (vertical) or \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\) (horizontal), both of which are\nnon\u2011sunny \u2013 a contradiction.  Hence \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains **at most two**\npoints of \\(S_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The non\u2011sunny line must contain exactly two points, lying on different diagonals\n\nBecause each line can meet each diagonal in at most one point, the total number\nof incidences equals \\(|S_{3}|=6\\).  With three lines, the average number of\npoints per line is \\(2\\); therefore each line contains exactly two points.\n\nThus \\(\\ell_{1}\\) contains two points belonging to **different** diagonals.\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) would keep the sum \\(a+b\\) constant, so it cannot\ncontain points from different diagonals.  Consequently \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is either\nvertical (\\(x=c\\)) or horizontal (\\(y=c\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Exhaustive analysis of the possible non\u2011sunny lines\n\n*Vertical case.*  \nThe only vertical lines meeting two points of \\(S_{3}\\) are\n\n\\[\nx=1:\\;(1,1),(1,2),\\qquad\nx=2:\\;(2,1),(2,2).\n\\]\n\n(The line \\(x=3\\) meets only \\((3,1)\\).)\n\n*Case \\(x=1\\).*  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nDiagonal \\(D_{4}\\) contributes the three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\); a sunny\nline can contain at most one of them.  Since we have only two sunny lines,\nat most two points of \\(D_{4}\\) can be covered, leaving at least one point of\n\\(D_{4}\\) uncovered \u2013 impossible.\n\n*Case \\(x=2\\).*  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(