give sunny lines:\n\n* \\(L_1\\): through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\));\n* \\(L_2\\): through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\));\n* \\(L_3\\): through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 2. Equations of the three sunny lines\n\nUsing point–slope form:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y-1 = 1\\,(x-1) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y = x,\\\\[2mm]\nL_2 &: y-2 = -\\tfrac12\\,(x-1) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2mm]\nL_3 &: y-3 = -2\\,(x-1) \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(1,\\ -\\tfrac12,\\ -2\\) are all finite and distinct from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); none is infinite. Consequently each line is sunny, and because the slopes differ the three lines are distinct.\n\n---\n\n#### 3. Coverage of all required points\n\nFrom the construction:\n\n* \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) lie on \\(L_1\\);\n* \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on \\(L_2\\);\n* \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) lie on \\(L_3\\).\n\nThus every point listed in (1) belongs to at least one of the three lines. No additional lines are needed, and we have exactly three distinct lines, all of which are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited a set of three distinct sunny lines that together contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\). Hence for \\(n=3\\) the integer  \n\n\\[\nk=3\n\\]\n\nis realizable.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=3\\text{ is possible for }n=3}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
