ts that can be supplied is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-2)\\times 1 = n-2 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 > n-2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   (the inequality is equivalent to \\(n-1>2\\), which holds).  \n   Thus the remaining points cannot be covered by only \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines – a contradiction.  \n\n   Therefore a covering with \\(n\\) lines cannot contain two or more sunny lines.  \n\n   Consequently the only admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) consists of the integer points of a right‑angled triangular region; its cardinality is\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx\\text{-axis }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\ny\\text{-axis }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\nx+y=0\\;( \\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nThus a sunny line has a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Existence of a covering with \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(c\\) lie on \\(L_c\\); conversely every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to exactly one such diagonal.  \nHence the family \\(\\{L_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (which contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines) covers \\(S_n\\).  \nEach \\(L_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is sunny.  \n\nThus a covering with **no** sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of a covering with \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_0:\\;y=x .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(L_0\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,2),\\dots ,\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor every integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) we keep the diagonal \\(L_c:x+y=c\\) **except** for one value of \\(c\\) (any one of them).  \nThus we have the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{L_0\\}\\cup\\{L_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1,\\;c\\neq c_0\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(c_0\\) is a fixed integer in \\([2,n+1]\\).  \nThe family contains exactly \\(1+(n-1)=n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a=b\\), it belongs to \\(L_0\\).  \nIf \\(a\\neq b\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\in[2,n+1]\\).  \nIf \\(c\\neq c_0\\) the point lies on the diagonal \\(L_c\\) which is in \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nIf \\(c=c_0\\) then \\(a\\neq b\\) (otherwise \\(c_0\\) would be even and the point \\((\\frac{c_0}{2},\\frac{c_0}{2})\\) would already be on \\(L_0\\)).  \nHence the point \\((a,b)\\) is still on the diagonal \\(L_{c_0}\\), which we have **removed**; however it is on the line \\(L_0\\) only when \\(a=b\\).  \nTo remedy this, choose \\(c_0\\) to be any odd integer (there is at least one because the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) contains an odd number for every \\(n\\ge 3\\)).  \nWhen \\(c_0\\) is odd, the diagonal \\(x+y=c_0\\) contains **no** point with equal coordinates, so every point of that diagonal has \\(a\\neq b\\) and is therefore uncovered.  \nTo cover those points we replace the missing diagonal by the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\;x= \\frac{c_0-1}{2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(V\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and it contains exactly the points of the diagonal \\(x+y=c_0\\) whose abscissa equals \\(\\frac{c_0-1}{2}\\); those are precisely the points \\(\\bigl(\\frac{c_0-1}{2},b\\bigr)\\) with \\(b=1,\\dots ,\\frac{c_0+1}{2}\\).  \nTogether with the already present diagonal lines for the other values of \\(c\\) and the sunny line \\(L_0\\) we obtain a covering of all points of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which (\\(L_0\\)) is sunny.\n\nThus a covering with **exactly one** sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Non‑existence of a covering with \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there is a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing at least two sunny lines.  \nSelect two sunny lines \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) from \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Intersection pattern.*  \nBecause a sunny line is not parallel to any diagonal \\(x+y=c\\), each of \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) meets every diagonal in exactly one point.  \nSince \\(U\\neq V\\), they intersect in at most one point of the plane; consequently on the diagonal that contains this intersection the two lines share the same point, while on each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonals they meet at distinct points.  \nHence the set \\(U\\cup V\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|U\\cup V| = 2n-1\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Points still uncovered.*  \nUsing (1),\n\n\\[\n|S_n\\setminus (U\\cup V)|\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-(2n-1)\n   =\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese points must be covered by the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\), all of which are non‑sunny.\n\n*Capability of a non‑sunny line.*  \nA non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or diagonal (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nIf it is **not** parallel to a given diagonal, it meets that diagonal in at most one point.  \nTherefore, on any diagonal that is not taken as a whole line, at most one of the \\(n-2\\) remaining lines can contribute a point to that diagonal.\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \nThe two sunny lines already provide one point on each diagonal; to cover the \\(\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) points left in (8) we would need, on average, more than one extra point per diagonal.  \nFormally, the total number of extra points that the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines can supply is at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-2)\\times 1 = n-2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBut for every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 > n-2 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, (10) is equivalent to \\(n-1>2\\).)\n\nThus the remaining \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines are insufficient to cover all points left after using the two sunny lines, a contradiction.\n\nConsequently no covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) lines can contain two or more sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\n* For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) a covering with **no** sunny line exists (diagonal family (2)).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) a covering with **exactly one** sunny line exists (family (5) together with the auxiliary vertical line (6)).  \n* No covering can contain **two or more** sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3,\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\text{ and only these values are possible}.}\n\\]"
