            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nThus a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and replace the line \\(x=n\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;(n,1)\\;(2,2).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{2-1}{2-n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,},\n\\]\n\nwhich is finite, non‑zero and different from \\(-1\\); consequently \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are still covered:\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* The point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(L\\).\n* The point \\((2,2)\\) also lies on \\(L\\) (it is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=2\\), but double coverage is allowed).\n\nHence we have a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  No configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition and contains at least two sunny lines.  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nV:=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal{F}\\},\\qquad\nN:=n-V\\;(\\text{non‑vertical lines}).\n\\]\n\nBecause each sunny line is non‑vertical, \\(k\\ge2\\) implies \\(N\\ge2\\) and therefore  \n\n\\[\nV\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Columns without a vertical line\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) denote by  \n\n\\[\nC_a:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of required points in column \\(a\\).  \nThe cardinality of \\(C_a\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|C_a|=n+1-a\\qquad (1\\le a\\le n). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf a vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\), then **all** points of \\(C_a\\) are already covered.  \nIf no vertical line with \\(x=a\\) is present, each point of \\(C_a\\) must be covered by a distinct non‑vertical line, because a non‑vertical line meets the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.\n\nConsequently, for every column lacking a vertical line we need at least \\(|C_a|\\) distinct non‑vertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Counting argument\n\nFrom (1) we have at least two columns without a vertical line (since \\(V\\le n-2\\)).  \nLet these columns be \\(a<b\\).  \nUsing (2),\n\n\\[\n|C_a|\\ge n+1-b+ (b-a)=|C_b|+(b-a)\\ge |C_b|+1 .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for any \\(n\\ge3\\) we have \\(|C_{n-1}|=2\\) and \\(|C_{n}|=1\\).\n\nBecause there are only \\(N=n-V\\le2\\) non‑vertical lines, the total number of incidences of non‑vertical lines with the two columns \\(a,b\\) is at most \\(2\\cdot2=4\\).\n\n*If \\(a=n\\)*, then \\(|C_a|=1\\) and \\(|C_b|\\ge2\\); the four incidences are sufficient only when \\(|C_b|=2\\) and each non‑vertical line meets both columns.  \nBut a non‑vertical line cannot intersect the same column twice, so each of the two non‑vertical lines can cover **at most one** point of column \\(b\\). Hence column \\(b\\) would need at least two distinct non‑vertical lines, which forces both non‑vertical lines to be used on column \\(b\\) and leaves no line to cover the point of column \\(a=n\\) – a contradiction.\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-1\\)*, then \\(|C_a|\\ge2\\).  \nSince each non‑vertical line can meet column \\(a\\) in at most one point, covering all points of column \\(a\\) already requires at least two non‑vertical lines.  \nThe second column \\(b\\) also needs at least one additional incidence, which would require a third non‑vertical line, contradicting \\(N\\le2\\).\n\nThus a configuration with at least two sunny (hence non‑vertical) lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Small values \\(n=3,4\\)\n\nThe above counting argument already covers all \\(n\\ge3\\); nevertheless we verify the impossibility of \\(k\\ge2\\) directly.\n\n*For \\(n=3\\):* the set \\(S_3\\) consists of six points. Any two sunny lines are non‑vertical, so at most one vertical line can be present. The column without a vertical line contains at least two points, which would need two distinct non‑vertical lines – exactly the two sunny lines – leaving the third column uncovered. Hence \\(k\\ge2\\) is impossible.\n\n*For \\(n=4\\):* \\(S_4\\) contains ten points. With at least two sunny lines we have at most two non‑vertical lines. At least two columns lack a vertical line; each of those columns contains at least two points, requiring at least four incidences, which again excee