   Therefore we must have \\(a\\ge n-1\\); the only possible columns without a vertical line are \\(a=n-1\\) and \\(a=n\\).  \n   Consequently \\(V=n-2\\) and the two missing vertical lines are exactly \\(x=n-1\\) and \\(x=n\\).\n\n   The three points that are not already covered by the vertical lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\qquad (n-1,1),\\qquad (n-1,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Let the two sunny lines be \\(L\\) and \\(M\\).  \n   Because a line can meet a given column in at most one point, one of the lines must contain \\((n,1)\\).  \n   Any line through \\((n,1)\\) and another point \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{y-1}{x-n}.\n   \\]\n\n   The slope is \\(0\\) exactly when \\(y=1\\) (horizontal, not sunny); it is \\(-1\\) exactly when \\(y=n+1-x\\) (the forbidden line \\(x+y=n+1\\)).  \n   Choosing a point that avoids both conditions (for instance \\((1,2)\\)) gives a genuine sunny line \\(L\\) passing through \\((n,1)\\).\n\n   The remaining two points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\) lie in the same column \\(x=n-1\\).  \n   A sunny line can meet this column in at most one point, so the second sunny line \\(M\\) can cover **at most one** of them.  \n   The other point would stay uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered.\n\n   Hence the assumption that there are two sunny lines is impossible.  We have proved  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   None of them is sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered and \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\).**  \n\n   *Case \\(n\\ge4\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and replace the line \\(x=n\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(\\displaystyle m=\\frac{2-1}{1-n}=-\\frac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n   Coverage check:  \n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   * The point \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  \n   * The point \\((1,2)\\) also lies on \\(\\ell\\) (it is already covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\), double coverage is allowed).  \n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines and exactly one line (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.\n\n   *Case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   Take the vertical lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\) together with  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;(3,1)\\text{ and }(1,2),\n   \\qquad m=\\frac{2-1}{1-3}=-\\frac12\\neq0,-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The same verification as above shows that all three points of \\(S_3\\) are covered and \\(\\ell\\) is the unique sunny line.\n\n   Thus a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   From (3) we have \\(k\\le1\\); the constructions in Sections 3 and 4 show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable.  Therefore the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
