            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and fully rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) let  \n   \\[\n   C_a=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\}=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\},\n   \\qquad |C_a|=n+1-a .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   *A line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and undefined (vertical).*\n\n   Let \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Denote  \n\n   * \\(V\\) – the number of vertical lines among \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n   * \\(N=n-V\\) – the number of non‑vertical lines.\n\n   For a fixed column \\(a\\) (i.e. the set \\(C_a\\)), a non‑vertical line meets that column in **at most one** point, because two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate would force the line to be vertical.  \n\n   Define  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=a\\text{ is **not** a vertical line of }\\mathcal L\\}.\n   \\]\n   Then \\(|A|=N\\) (each column either has a vertical line or belongs to \\(A\\)).  \n\n   Counting the incidences “(non‑vertical line, column in \\(A\\))’’ in two ways gives  \n\n   \\[\n   N\\cdot |A|\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{a\\in A}|C_a|\n   \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   N^{2}\\ge\\sum_{a\\in A}(n+1-a). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The right–hand side of (1) is maximised when the columns in \\(A\\) are the smallest possible, i.e. \\(A=\\{1,2,\\dots ,N\\}\\).  In that case  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{a\\in A}(n+1-a)=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\bigl(n+1-i\\bigr)\n   =N(n+1)-\\frac{N(N+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting into (1) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   N^{2}\\ge N(n+1)-\\frac{N(N+1)}2\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   \\frac{3N+1}{2}\\ge n+1\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   N\\ge\\frac{2n+1}{3}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (2) is a **lower** bound on the total number of non‑vertical lines; it will be used only to guarantee that if there are two sunny lines then at least two columns belong to \\(A\\).\n\n   Among the \\(N\\) non‑vertical lines, those that are **not** sunny are exactly the horizontal lines (slope \\(0\\)) and the lines of slope \\(-1\\).  Consequently the number of sunny lines is at most the number of non‑vertical lines that are neither horizontal nor of slope \\(-1\\).\n\n   **Claim.**  At most one sunny line can appear in any covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that there are at least two sunny lines.  Then \\(N\\ge2\\); by (2) we have \\(N\\ge2\\) automatically, so at least two columns belong to \\(A\\).  Let  \n\n   \\[\n   a_0=\\min A .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a_0\\) is a column without a vertical line, all points of \\(C_{a_0}\\) must be supplied by non‑vertical lines, and each such line contributes at most one point of \\(C_{a_0}\\).  Since there are at least two sunny lines, there are at least two non‑vertical lines that are not forced to be horizontal or slope \\(-1\\); each of them can cover at most one point of \\(C_{a_0}\\).\n\n   If \\(a_0\\le n-2\\) then \\(|C_{a_0}|=n+1-a_0\\ge3\\).  Two non‑vertical lines can cover at most two points of \\(C_{a_0}\\), leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.  Hence we must have  \n\n   \\[\n   a_0\\ge n-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a_0\\le n\\), the only possible columns without a vertical line are \\(x=n-1\\) and \\(x=n\\).  Consequently \\(V=n-2\\) and the two missing vertical lines are exactly \\(x=n-1\\) and \\(x=n\\).\n\n   The points of \\(S_n\\) that are not already covered by the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n,1),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Two sunny lines must cover these three points.  A sunny line can meet a given column in at most one point, so one of the sunny lines must contain \\((n,1)\\) (the only point in column \\(x=n\\)).  The second sunny line can meet column \\(x=n-1\\) in at most one point, therefore it can cover at most one of \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  The remaining point would stay uncovered – again a contradiction.\n\n   Hence the assumption that there are two sunny lines is impossible, and the claim follows.  ∎\n\n   From the claim we obtain the universal upper bound  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n   \\]\n\n   None of them is sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.  This shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n4. **Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\).**  \n\n   *Construction for all \\(n\\ge3\\).*  \n   Use the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-1\n   \\]\n\n   (these are \\(n-1\\) lines, none sunny) and replace the line \\(x=n\\) by the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell:\\;(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,2).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\frac{2-1}{1-n}=-\\frac{1}{\\,n-1\\,},\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and undefined; thus \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n\n   **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   * If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); the only possible point is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(\\ell\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly one line (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.  The construction works for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (for \\(n=3\\) the set of vertical lines is \\(x=1, x=2\\) and \\(\\ell\\) is the line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no covering family can contain more than one sunny line, while Sections 3 and 4 provide explicit families attaining \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  Therefore the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
