,n\\}\\).\n\n3. **Proof of Lemma 1 (induction on \\(m\\)).**  \n\n   *Base case \\(m=3\\).*  \n   The three points of \\(S_{3}\\) are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:y=2x-1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}:y=3x-1,\\qquad\n   \\ell_{3}:y=5x-9\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(2,3,5\\) (none equals \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)) and each passes through a distinct point of \\(S_{3}\\); consequently they cover all three points.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the statement holds for some \\(m-1\\ge 3\\); i.e. there are sunny lines\n   \\(\\mathcal L_{m-1}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\}\\) covering \\(S_{m-1}\\).\n\n   Consider \\(S_{m}\\).  Define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{m}:\\;(m,1)\\text{ and }(1,m-1)\n   \\qquad\\text{(slope }s_{m}=-(m-2)/(m-1)\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\text{)} .\n   \\]\n\n   Remove the two points \\((m,1)\\) and \\((1,m-1)\\) from \\(S_{m}\\).  \n   The remaining set splits into\n\n   * the column \\(x=1\\) with points \\((1,b)\\) for \\(1\\le b\\le m-2\\);\n   * the translated triangle\n     \\[\n     S_{m}^{\\prime}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n   By the change of variables \\(a'=a-1\\) we have a bijection\n   \\[\n   (a,b)\\longleftrightarrow (a',b),\\qquad a'+b\\le m,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(S_{m}^{\\prime}\\) is a copy of \\(S_{m-1}\\).\n\n   Apply the induction hypothesis to \\(S_{m-1}\\) and obtain the sunny lines\n   \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\) covering it.  Translate each of them one unit to the\n   right (replace \\(x\\) by \\(x-1\\)); denote the translated lines by\n   \\(L_{1}^{+},\\dots ,L_{m-1}^{+}\\).  Translation does not change the slope,\n   therefore all \\(L_{i}^{+}\\) are still sunny.\n\n   *Coverage of the translated triangle.*  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in S_{m}^{\\prime}\\) we have \\((a-1,b)\\in S_{m-1}\\); by the\n   induction hypothesis some \\(L_{i}\\) contains \\((a-1,b)\\), hence the\n   translated line \\(L_{i}^{+}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *Coverage of the column \\(x=1\\).*  \n   Each original line \\(L_{i}\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=0\\) in exactly one\n   point (because it is not vertical).  After translation the line \\(L_{i}^{+}\\)\n   meets the line \\(x=1\\) in the same point, which belongs to the set\n   \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m-2\\}\\).  Consequently the \\(m-1\\) translated lines\n   together cover every point of the column \\(x=1\\) except possibly \\((1,m-1)\\),\n   which is already covered by \\(\\ell_{m}\\).\n\n   Thus the family\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{m}:=\\{L_{1}^{+},\\dots ,L_{m-1}^{+},\\ell_{m}\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_{m}\\).\n   This completes the induction.\n\n   Hence Lemma 1 holds for every \\(m\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); none is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the\n   sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-1/(n-1)\\)).  All points\n   are covered, exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  For \\(n\\ge 3\\) take the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines\n   \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and apply Lemma 1 with \\(m=2\\) to the remaining triangle\n   \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  An explicit covering with two\n   sunny lines is\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{1}:\\;(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(1,n-1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_{2}:\\;(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,n-1),\n   \\]\n   both of which have slopes different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\).\n\n   *General \\(k\\) (\\(3\\le k\\le n\\)).*  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) and apply Lemma 1 to the\n   right‑hand triangle of size \\(k\\).  Lemma 1 supplies exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   lines covering that triangle; together we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines covering all of \\(S_{n}\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration\n   exists.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll non‑negative integers \\(k\\) not exceeding \\(n\\) are attainable, and no larger\n\\(k\\) is possible because we have only \\(n\\) lines in total.  Therefore the set\nof admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
