-j))=(1,j)\\).  \n   – Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\). Set \\(i=a\\). Then \\(L_i\\) contains \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,n+2-a)\\). The line \\(L_i\\) has equation\n   \\[\n   y-1 = s_i\\,(x-i),\\qquad s_i=-\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}.\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\((a,b)\\) gives\n   \\[\n   b-1 = -\\frac{n+1-a}{a-1}\\,(a-a)=0,\n   \\]\n   which is false in general; therefore the interior points are **not** automatically on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n   To cover the interior points we use the following observation: for any \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\) belong to the set, and the line through them is precisely \\(L_{a}\\) (because \\(b=n+2-a\\) is equivalent to \\(a+b=n+2\\)).  \n   Hence every interior point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and therefore lies on the line that joins \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b')\\) where \\(b'=n+2-a\\ge b\\). Since the line \\(L_a\\) contains **all** points \\((a, y)\\) with \\(1\\le y\\le n+2-a\\), in particular it contains \\((a,b)\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n   Thus we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n6. **Partial conclusion.**  \n   From the three constructions we have proved that the values\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\n   \\]\n   are attainable, while \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n   The existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(k=3,4,\\dots ,n-1\\) has not been settled in the present write‑up.\n\n--- \n\n**What remains open:**  \nA complete solution would require a construction (or a proof of impossibility) for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n-1\\). The difficulty lies in arranging exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines to cover the triangular lattice \\(S_n\\) while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are forced to be vertical (or horizontal) and therefore non‑sunny. A successful construction mus