wo sunny lines would have to cover \\(S_2\\) with two sunny lines\u201d is invalid, breaking the chain of reasoning.\n\n**Effect on Subsequent Steps:** Since the impossibility claim for \\(k=2\\) is unsupported, any later conclusions that rely on this claim are not justified.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Construction for \\(k=0\\) (Step\u202f3)\n\n> \u201cTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\). Every point of \\(S_n\\) has integer abscissa between \\(1\\) and \\(n\\), therefore it lies on one of these lines. All the lines are vertical, hence none of them is sunny. This gives a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n\n- For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n- Vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, thus not sunny.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This construction is correct and fully justifies the existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Construction for \\(k=1\\) (Step\u202f4)\n\n> \u201cUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the single sunny line \\(\\ell:\\; y = (n-1)\\,x-(n-1)^2\\). The line \\(\\ell\\) passes through the point \\((n,1)\\) (because \\(1=(n-1)n-(n-1)^2\\)) and its slope is \\(n-1\\neq0,-1\\). All points of \\(S_n\\) whose abscissa is \\(n\\) are of the form \\((n,1)\\); they are covered by \\(\\ell\\). Every other point has abscissa at most \\(n-1\\) and is therefore covered by one of the vertical lines. Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\u201d\n\n**Verification of the Claim \u201c\\(\\ell\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\)\u201d**\n\nCompute the right\u2011hand side of the line equation at \\(x=n\\):\n\\[\ny = (n-1)n-(n-1)^2 = (n^2-n)-(n^2-2n+1)= -n+2n-1 = n-1.\n\\]\nThus the point on \\(\\ell\\) with \\(x=n\\) is \\((n,n-1)\\), **not** \\((n,1)\\).\n\nConsequences:\n\n- The point \\((n,1)\\) (which belongs to \\(S_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge2\\)) is **not** covered by \\(\\ell\\).  \n- The only vertical line with \\(x=n\\) has been omitted from the set of lines; therefore \\((n,1)\\) remains uncovered.  \n\nHence the proposed configuration fails to satisfy the covering condition.\n\n**Classification:** **Critical Error** \u2013 the line \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the required point, invalidating the entire construction for \\(k=1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Construction for \\(k=n\\) (Step\u202f5)\n\nThe solution proceeds in several sub\u2011steps. Each is examined in turn.\n\n#### 5.1 Definition of the lines\n\n> \u201cFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line \\(L_i\\) through the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,n+2-i)\\).  \n> The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(s_i = -\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\) for \\(2\\le i\\le n\\); for \\(i=1\\) we take the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,n)\\), whose slope is \\(\\frac{n-1}{1}=n-1\\). Consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\u201d\n\n**Verification of the slope claim**\n\n- For \\(i\\ge2\\) the slope formula is correct:  \n  \\[\n  s_i = \\frac{1-(n+2-i)}{i-1}= -\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}.\n  \\]\n\n- **Slope \\(-1\\) possibility:**  \n  Solve \\(-\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1} = -1\\) \u2192 \\(\\frac{n+1-i}{i-1}=1\\) \u2192 \\(n+1-i = i-1\\) \u2192 \\(2i = n+2\\) \u2192 \\(i = \\frac{n+2}{2}\\).  \n\n  If \\(n\\) is **even**, \\(\\frac{n+2}{2}\\) is an integer between \\(2\\) and \\(n\\). For that value of \\(i\\) the slope equals \\(-1\\), which is **not** allowed for a sunny line.  \n\n  Example: \\(n=4\\). Then \\(i = 3\\). The line through \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) has slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n- **Slope \\(0\\) possibility:**  \n  \\(s_i = 0\\) would require \\(n+1-i = 0\\) \u2192 \\(i = n+1\\), impossible because \\(i\\le n\\).  \n\n- **Infinite slope (vertical) possibility:**  \n  Occurs only when \\(i=1\\) (denominator zero). The solution treats \\(i=1\\) separately, using 