            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a positive integer \\(m\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_m\\) consists of the integer points inside the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(m,1),(1,m)\\); it contains \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{m(m+1)}2\\) points.\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); equivalently the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of sunny lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines that together cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Sunny covering of a triangular lattice  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \n*For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(S_m\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWe argue by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n*Base \\(m=1\\).*  \n\\(S_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\). The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) is sunny and contains the only point. Hence the statement holds.\n\n*Induction step.*  \nAssume the lemma true for some \\(m-1\\ge 1\\); i.e. there are \\(m-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_{m-1}\\).  \nAmong those \\(m-1\\) lines at most one can pass through the point \\((1,m)\\) (otherwise two distinct lines would coincide). If one already passes through \\((1,m)\\) we are done, because the same family of \\(m-1\\) lines also covers \\(S_m\\) (the extra points of \\(S_m\\) that are not in \\(S_{m-1}\\) are precisely \\((1,m)\\) and \\((m,1)\\); the latter belongs to \\(S_{m-1}\\)).  \n\nOtherwise none of the \\(m-1\\) lines contains \\((1,m)\\).  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_m:\\quad\\text{through }(1,m)\\text{ and }(2,1).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-m}{2-1}=1-m,\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\) for any \\(m\\ge 2\\); hence \\(\\ell_m\\) is sunny.  \nThe line \\(\\ell_m\\) is distinct from all previously constructed lines because none of them contains the point \\((1,m)\\).  \n\nNow the \\(m\\) lines (the \\(m-1\\) from the induction hypothesis together with \\(\\ell_m\\)) cover  \n\n* all points of \\(S_{m-1}\\) (by the induction hypothesis), and  \n* the point \\((1,m)\\) (by construction of \\(\\ell_m\\)).  \n\nThe remaining point of \\(S_m\\) that is not in \\(S_{m-1}\\) is \\((m,1)\\); it already belongs to \\(S_{m-1}\\) because \\(m+1\\le (m-1)+1+1\\). Consequently every point of \\(S_m\\) is covered.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(m\\), completing the induction. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reducing the problem to a smaller triangle  \n\n#### Lemma 2  \n*Let \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\). There exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) such that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.*\n\n**Proof.**  \nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct and are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) fails to lie on any of those vertical lines precisely when \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\).  \nFor such a point we have  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (a-(n-k)) + b \\le k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence the set of uncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(a',b)\\mid a'\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\).  In other words, after the translation  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\longmapsto (a-(n-k),\\,b)\n\\]\n\nthe set \\(T\\) becomes exactly \\(S_k\\).\n\nBy Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that cover all points of \\(S_k\\).  \nTranslating those lines back by \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x+n-k,\\,y)\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering the original set \\(T\\).\n\nTogether with the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines we now have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(S_n\\); precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Determination of all possible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Lemma 2 we have exhibited, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration satisfying the two conditions of the problem.  \n\nConversely, any admissible configuration uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
