in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently it lies on exactly one of the listed diagonals.  \nThus the required configuration exists for \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)  \n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;\\dots ,\\;x+y=n\n\\]\n\n(all non‑sunny).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) are already covered.  \nThe remaining points are those with \\(a+b=n+1\\); they all lie on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nChoose any sunny line that passes through at least one of those points; for instance the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-1)\\) has slope \\(-\\frac12\\), which is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\).  \nThat sunny line contains the whole diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) (because any two points of the diagonal determine the same line), so it covers all the uncovered points.  \nHence a configuration with exactly one sunny line exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n#### 2.3  \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)  \n\nWe exhibit \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\text{ the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,i).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{1-i}{i-1}= -\\frac{1}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i)\\) are distinct for different values of \\(i\\), so the lines \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nIf \\(n\\) is even, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}=\\{L_1,L_2,\\dots ,L_{n-1},\\;x= \\tfrac{n}{2}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) sunny lines (the vertical line \\(x=\\tfrac{n}{2}\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or \\(x+y=0\\) because its slope is \\(\\infty\\); however a vertical line **is** parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence it is *non‑sunny*.  \nTo keep all lines sunny we replace the vertical line by the line through \\((\\tfrac{n}{2},1)\\) and \\((1,\\tfrac{n}{2})\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac{1}{\\tfrac{n}{2}-1}\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\).  \nThus for even \\(n\\) we have exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf \\(n\\) is odd, add the line  \n\n\\[\nL_n:\\text{ the line through }\\Bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr)\\text{ and }(1,n).\n\\]\n\nIts slope is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n-\\frac{n+1}{2}}{1-\\frac{n+1}{2}}=\n\\frac{\\frac{n-1}{2}}{-\\frac{n-1}{2}}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is *not* sunny.  \nInstead we take the line through \\(\\bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2},1\\bigr)\\) and \\((1,\\frac{n+1}{2})\\); its slope is \\(-\\frac{1}{\\frac{n-1}{2}}\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\).  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1},L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\nEvery point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to exactly one of these lines: a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le b\\) lies on the line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,a)\\); a point with \\(a>b\\) lies on the line through \\((b,1)\\) and \\((1,b)\\).  \nHence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered, and we have a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Impossibility when \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where  \n\n\\[\n2\\le k\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nLet the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines be denoted by \\(\\mathcal{N}\\).  \nEach line of \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) is of one of the three types:\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nConsequently any two points that share the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate or the same sum \\(x+y\\) cannot lie on a sunny line together; a line through such a pair would be parallel to a line of \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) and therefore non‑sunny.\n\nHence **a sunny line can contain at most one point from each vertical line, each horizontal line and each diagonal**.\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) involves at most \\(n-k\\) distinct vertical lines, at most \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal lines and at most \\(n-k\\) distinct diagonals; altogether at most \\(3(n-k)\\) different “directions’’ (rows, columns, diagonals) are represented among the non‑sunny lines.\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct rows \\(\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\}\\), \\(n\\) distinct columns \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\}\\) and \\(n\\) distinct diagonals \\(\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(k\\ge2\\), we have \\(n-k\\le n-2\\); therefore at least two rows, two columns and two diagonals are **absent** from \\(\\mathcal{N}\\).\n\nChoose a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) whose row, column and diagonal are all absent from \\(\\mathcal{N}\\) (such a point exists by the pigeon‑hole principle).  \nNo non‑sunny line passes through \\((a,b)\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) must be covered by a sunny line, say \\(\\ell\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, it cannot be parallel to any of the missing rows, columns or diagonals.  \nTherefore \\(\\ell\\) cannot contain any other point of \\(S_n\\) that shares with \\((a,b)\\) either the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, the same \\(y\\)-coordinate or the same sum \\(x+y\\).  \nBut **every** other point of \\(S_n\\) shares at least one of these three attributes with \\((a,b)\\): indeed, for any \\((c,d)\\in S_n\\) we have either \\(c=a\\) (same column), \\(d=b\\) (same row) or \\(c+d=a+b\\) (same diagonal).  \nHence \\(\\ell\\) contains **only** the point \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(S_n\\) that is not already covered by \\(\\mathcal{N}\\).  \nSince the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines miss at least three points of \\(S_n\\) (the point \\((a,b)\\) and at least two others, because \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\)), we need at least three sunny lines to cover the uncovered points.  \nBut we have only \\(k\\le n-1\\) sunny lines, and for \\(k\\ge2\\) the inequality  \n\n\\[\nk < \\text{(number of uncovered points)}\n\\]\n\nholds, a contradiction.\n\nTherefore no configuration exists when \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe three lemmas provide explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\); the impossibility argument rules out every other non‑negative integer \\(k\\).  \nHence the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]"
