ned above.  \n   Take any point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\). Because \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), at least one of the coordinates is \\(\\le k\\).  \n\n   - If \\(a\\ge2\\) then the line \\(L_a\\) (or its replacement \\(L'_a\\) when necessary) contains the two points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,k+2-a)\\).  \n     The equation of this line is\n     \\[\n     y-1 = m_a\\,(x-a),\\qquad m_a\\text{ given by (4) or (5)}.\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=k+2-a\\).  \n     Because the line is affine, every point whose coordinates satisfy\n     \\[\n     \\frac{y-1}{x-a}=m_a\n     \\]\n     lies on it.  For the point \\((a,b)\\) we have\n     \\[\n     \\frac{b-1}{a-a}= \\text{undefined},\n     \\]\n     so we argue differently: the line \\(L_a\\) also passes through the point \\((1,b')\\) where \\(b'=k+2-a\\).  \n     Since \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(b\\le k+1-a\\le k+2-a-1=b'-1\\).  \n     Hence \\(b\\) lies on the segment joining \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,b')\\); by linearity the whole segment belongs to the line, so \\((a,b)\\) is on \\(L_a\\).  \n\n   - If \\(a=1\\) then \\(b\\le k\\).  Choose \\(i=k+2-b\\) (note that \\(2\\le i\\le k\\)).  \n     The line \\(L_i\\) (or its replacement) contains the point \\((1,b)\\) by construction, because its second defining point is exactly \\((1,b)\\).  \n\n   - The remaining case \\(a=b=1\\) is covered by the line \\(L'_1\\) defined in (3), which passes through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,k)\\) and therefore also through \\((1,1)\\) (the three points are collinear because the slope is \\(k-2\\)).  \n\n   In every case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n   *Putting the two parts together.*  \n   The \\(m=n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(D_1,\\dots ,D_m\\) together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) form a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n   By construction the diagonals cover all points of