im that the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers all of \\(T_k\\).\n\n#### 3.4  Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(T_k\\)\n\nThe solution attempts to prove coverage by case analysis.\n\n- **Case “\\(a\\ge2\\)”:**  \n  *Claim:* Choose \\(i=a\\); then the line (either \\(L_i\\) or \\(L'_i\\)) contains the points \\(P_i=(a,1)\\) and \\(Q_i=(1,k+2-a)\\). The solution asserts that any point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k+2-a-1\\) lies on the segment joining these two endpoints, hence on the line.\n\n  - **Verification:** The line through \\((a,1)\\) and \\((1,k+2-a)\\) is not vertical; its equation is  \n    \\[\n    y-1 = -\\frac{k+1-a}{a-1}\\,(x-a).\n    \\]  \n    For a point with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(x=a\\) we obtain \\(y=1\\) uniquely. Thus **no** point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\) lies on this line. The only points of the line having integer coordinates are the two endpoints (and possibly some interior points when the slope is a rational number with denominator dividing \\(a-1\\)), but certainly not all points \\((a,b)\\) with the same \\(a\\). Hence the claim that “\\((a,b)\\) lies on the segment” is false.  \n\n  - **Classification:** **Critical Error** – the argument misapplies convex combinations; the line does not contain the whole vertical column at \\(x=a\\).\n\n- **Case “\\(a=1\\)”:**  \n  *Claim:* For \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\le k\\), set \\(i=k+2-b\\); then \\((1,b)\\) equals the point \\(Q_i\\) and thus lies on the corresponding line.  \n\n  - **Verification:** This part is correct **provided** the line \\(L_i\\) (or its replacement) indeed exists and is sunny. However, because the construction of the lines fails for \\(i=1\\) (see Issue 1), the point \\((1,1)\\) is not covered. Moreover, for other values of \\(i\\) the line may be \\(L_i\\) (which is fine) or \\(L'_i\\) (also fine), so the argument works for all \\(b\\ge2\\) but fails for \\(b=1\\).\n\n- **Case “\\((a,b)=(1,1)\\)”:**  \n  *Claim:* \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L'_1\\) because t